/S /P
/P 43 0 R
/E (A Freeport \(also known as a free trade zone or customs-free zone\) is a designated economic zone inside a country. Companies import raw materials into these zones without paying any import duties. Inside the hub, workers process the goods into finished products. Businesses then re-export the final items globally, so they pay zero tariffs throughout the production process. Governments build freeports around major ports and airports to attract foreign direct investment. They also offer slashed corporate tax rates and simplified regulations to create local manufacturing jobs. Cities like Dubai and Singapore use this strategy, and they have transformed into major trade hubs. At the same time, critics point out serious drawbacks. Companies sometimes use freeports for tax avoidance, and wealthy individuals store art and gold in opaque vaults to bypass regulatory scrutiny. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
