/P 45 0 R
/E (In Hindu mythology, Samudra Manthan \(the churning of the ocean\) represents the search for Amrit \(the nectar of immortality\). However, before the nectar emerged, the churning released Halahal, a deadly poison that threatened to destroy the world. Modern financial markets follow the exact same pattern. Whenever investors look for high returns, they must navigate market volatility, fraud, and sharp drops first. For example, high-risk assets like crypto, high-yield bonds, or early-stage startups can offer great rewards, but they also bring extreme downside risks. Just as Lord Shiva drank the poison to save the world, smart investors use risk management, stop-losses, and hedging strategies to protect their portfolios. To gain long-term wealth, you must respect the process, handle the dangerous spikes first, and remain patient until the true value emerges. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
